Original commit body from 9508f08a8f5e6d29d19eacff037260298e653344:

A fully annotated, unambiguous receiver produced no I/O boundary at all:

    import pathlib
    def h(p: pathlib.Path, x):
        return p.write_text(x)     # -> python:external:...:write_text, 0 boundaries

The annotation names the receiver's type exactly and the catalogue holds
pathlib.Path.write_text as fs_write, but the analyzer never routed the
annotation into the symbol id's module slot, so the edge arrived carrying the
`external` placeholder and gate_named_entry correctly refused it as an untyped
method call. Same for bare-with-from-import, aliased module imports, and
socket.socket.

Measured: poetry 38 -> 52 reported boundaries (+14), sqlalchemy 43 -> 47. The
additions are pathlib.Path.{exists,is_dir,is_file,stat,glob,iterdir,mkdir} on
annotated parameters. Three adjudicated against source, all true positives --
e.g. poetry's `def is_dir_writable(path: Path, create: bool = False)` calling
path.exists() and path.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True).

SCOPED TO PARAMETERS, on measurement. Over six Python repos: 95,245 method-call
edges carry the `external` placeholder; 4,160 have a receiver with a resolvable
annotation; 63 actually reach the catalogue -- and 96% of that payload is the
annotated-parameter shape alone. AnnAssign, return-annotated factories,
attribute reads and generics contribute exactly zero. Both exclusions are pinned
by tests that state why, so a later PR that routes them argues with a test
rather than silently widening the trust surface.

THE BINDING CHECK IS THE DESIGN, NOT A PRECAUTION. This is the first change in
this subsystem that ADDS classifications. A minted hint is trusted -- it bypasses
both gate_named_entry and the ambiguous_names net by design, and gating the
hinted path was measured to destroy 61.5-87.2% of all reported boundaries for
zero gain, so there is no backstop. A wrong hint is a confident false boundary
AND a false taint sink, never silence.

Emitting the RAW annotation text -- what the tree-sitter-side resolver does for
dotted annotations -- was measured to produce confirmed false boundaries:
`conn: Connection` matched sqlite3.Connection.execute and minted a
database-zone taint sink, with 174 such annotations in the corpus, because
_module_matches accepts an unqualified reference as a component suffix.
Resolved through its import binding the same annotation yields
sqlalchemy.engine.Connection, which does not match. All eight negative tests are
measured FP classes (the sqlalchemy collision, `from fastapi import Path`, an
unimported dotted root, an in-file `class Path` shadow, a forward-reference
string, Optional[...]); all eight passed before this change and must keep
passing.

Resolution reuses _import_binding_for (INV-kipor, same release), so "does an
import in this file rebind this name, and to what?" has one answer serving three
callers instead of drifting into a sixth private copy. An alias expands
(`import pathlib as pl`); an unbound name stays `external`; a parameter already
resolved to an in-repo class wins, so a first-party `class Path` is never
overridden by a same-named import.

NOTHING IS DONE FOR GO, and that is measured rather than deferred. Go has no
equivalent gap: 96.7% (3,618/3,741) of package-qualified-receiver catalogue-name
call sites already reach a usable module slot. Go's residual gap is initializer
type inference, and the candidate fix is net-harmful -- 118 of 692 moved edges
(17.1%) land on the wrong in-repo symbol, 66 of those on a struct field, for +8
boundaries on a 1,309 baseline (+0.61%). A single shared producer mechanism is
the wrong factoring; the Go gap is filed with its hijack measurement.

DISCLOSED GAPS: this is a cold start -- 17 of the 18 catalogue entries it
activates fire zero times on the corpus today, so their boundary labels have
never been validated against real output. Path.{exists,is_dir,is_file,stat} are
catalogued fs_read though they stat rather than read contents; that is a
catalogue-labelling question, untouched here. Relative imports, importlib,
conditional and TYPE_CHECKING imports are unprobed. The 63-site population is a
property of the six-repo bag, not of the tool: adding one repo moved it +20.6%,
and per-repo the change ranges +9.3% (sqlalchemy) to +36.8% (poetry).

